## Proof

**Setup.** Let $S \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a strictly convex surface bounding a strictly convex closed region $K$, and let $N: S \to S^2$ be the Gauss map sending each point $p \in S$ to its outward unit normal. The derivative $dN_p : T_p S \to T_{N(p)} S^2$ is the **shape operator** (Weingarten map) at $p$. Its eigenvalues are the principal curvatures $\kappa_1(p), \kappa_2(p)$, and

$$\det(dN_p) = \kappa_1(p)\,\kappa_2(p) = K(p),$$

the Gaussian curvature. Thus $dN_p$ is invertible if and only if $K(p) \neq 0$, i.e., both principal curvatures are nonzero.

**Strict convexity forces positive principal curvatures.** Since $K$ is a strictly convex body, for every boundary point $p$ and every tangent direction $v \in T_p S$, the surface lies strictly on one side of its tangent plane at $p$. Equivalently, the second fundamental form $\mathrm{I\!I}_p(v,v)$ (computed with the outward normal) is **positive definite** at every $p$:

$$\mathrm{I\!I}_p(v,v) > 0 \quad \text{for all } v \neq 0.$$

Indeed, if $\mathrm{I\!I}_p(v,v) \leq 0$ for some direction $v$, the surface would not curve away from the tangent plane in that direction, contradicting strict convexity of the enclosed region. Positive definiteness of $\mathrm{I\!I}_p$ means both principal curvatures satisfy

$$\kappa_1(p) > 0, \qquad \kappa_2(p) > 0 \quad \text{for all } p \in S.$$

**Conclusion.** Since $\kappa_1(p), \kappa_2(p) > 0$ everywhere,

$$\det(dN_p) = \kappa_1(p)\,\kappa_2(p) > 0 \quad \text{for all } p \in S.$$

A linear map on a 2-dimensional space is invertible precisely when its determinant is nonzero. Therefore $dN_p$ is invertible at every point $p \in S$.

**Answer:** Yes. The Gauss map on a strictly convex surface has an invertible derivative everywhere; in fact the Gaussian curvature is strictly positive at every point, so the shape operator $dN_p$ is a linear isomorphism for all $p \in S$.

